  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 111 AM CDT Tue Oct 6 2026\n\nWinds will turn southwesterly with increasing breezes into the \nafternoon as a fast-moving shortwave zips across southern\nOntario. Forecast soundings suggest some occasional 25-30 mph\ngusts will be possible this afternoon, particularly across far\nnorthern Illinois/close to the Wisconsin state line. Dewpoint/RH\ntrends remain a bit tricky as a plume of very dry 850-700 mb air\nwill meander across the forecast area today. Diurnal mixing is\nprogged to be deepest across our south, and have lowered\ndewpoints towards 40 based on the better performing guidance of\nlate which results in min RH values either side of 30 percent,\nwith higher values closer to the WI/IL state line. Winds look\nlight enough within the driest airmass to preclude an elevated\nfire danger at this point. \n\nAn attendant cold front will approach the region late tonight.\nAhead of the northwest wind shift, it's not out the question\nsome shallow mist/fog develops across far northwest Illinois\nprior to daybreak. Coverage and magnitude of any BR looks too\nlow to include in the grids at this time, however. In the wake\nof the front, modest cold advection will drive northwesterly\nbreezes across the region through Wednesday morning and\nafternoon. Still fairly deep mixing should allow temperatures to\nclimb back into the 70s, and perhaps near 80 in our far south. \n\nHigh pressure returns with a modest cool down on Thursday before\nstrengthening southwesterlies drive a warming trend into the\nweekend. Robust warm advection will develop Friday night into\nSaturday and some guidance (GFS) continues to squeeze out a\nlittle light precipitation. Associated soundings don't look\noverly supportive of this, however, and sub-mentionable PoPs\nfrom the NBM seem appropriate at this time. \n\nSaturday looks dry, warm, and breezy, with highs potentially \nmaking a run into the lower 80s south of about I-80 (and mid- \nupper 70s elsewhere). There is some uncertainty by Sunday \nregarding the proximity of an area of rain associated with \ntropical moisture/remnants interacting with a broad upper low \npushing out of the ArkLaMiss. The extended multi-model consensus\ncontinues to suggest that this activity will remain off to our \nsouth and east, and given the expansiveness and magnitude of the\ndry airmass overhead, will continue to show precip-free \nconditions in the area. Assuming precip and clouds remain \nshunted to our southeast, Sunday will be another warm day with \nhighs in the upper 70s and lower 80s even up to the lake. \n\nShortwave ridging is forecast to build to our south early next\nweek, and we'll remain on its northern fringes. While this may \nopen us up to some low precip chances, most activity currently \nlooks like it'll remain displaced just to our north. \n\nCarlaw",
